#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""The fleet git tools CLI:  a thin client for the git tools of the hosted simple-tools MCP.

Every operation is remote.  The endpoints are the fixed constants below (no environment override) and the
bearer is the CLI user's OWN credential:  it comes from SIMPLE_TOOLS_BEARER in the environment (automation)
or one hidden interactive prompt (no echo - never an argument, history, a file or a log line here), and
there is no direct path, no credential minting and no other credential source.  The server, never this
client, decides who the bearer is and what it may do.

The tool surface is DISCOVERED at run time:  every list/show/call first performs tools/list on this caller's
own transport (bounded pages, cursor-loop rejection, each advertised schema validated) and only then acts.
The shipped catalog.json is an offline fixture for tests and reference, never call authority.  Read/write is
NOT discovered:  an action is a write unless the pinned, reviewed read set below names it.

Writes name their actor and this client resolves the actor for reads AND writes the same way:  a declared
seat or an explicit identity must match the seat the server itself reports for this bearer (the identities
answer), any mismatch is refused before the target call, and the resolved seat is injected as `identity`.
There is no flag that picks a seat, a token or an endpoint.

An unknown write outcome is never replayed automatically:  a dead answer after the request left, a 5xx/408 on
a write, or the server's outcome_unknown all print the uncertainty note and stop.  The server's own message
text is untrusted (it can echo caller input), so only its allow-listed error code is ever shown.  A genuine
relay refusal keeps its real code - it is never reclassified as an uncertain write.

clone, fetch, pull, ls-remote, bundle and archive run the local git program against the hosted read-only route, on the
same host as the MCP endpoint.  The server reads the repo as this bearer's own GitHub App and keeps the GitHub token, so
this client never holds one.  That route has no push.  publish reads local files and sends them through the same write
tool as `call`, so a caller need not pass a large file as an inline argument.

This file is standard library only.  The command environment prefix is _ENV_STEM below;
everything user-visible takes the installed name from argv[0].  Python 3.10+.
"""

